PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B}

The question requires identifying the single inaccurate statement about post-Vatican II scholarly treatments of Augustine on free will / voluntas / grace.  

- A, D, E, and F are all defensible readings of either Augustine’s own texts or the cited interpreters (Gilson on efficacious but non-Jansenist grace; the inseparability of philosophy and theology in Augustine; the De libero arbitrio dialogue with Evodius; and later Calvinist-style predestinarian readings).  
- C is likewise acceptable: Augustine’s treatment of bona voluntas in De libero arbitrio I does echo Stoic (including Senecan) psychology in treating voluntas as a distinct psychic power.  
- B, however, mischaracterizes R. A. Markus. Markus’s account of asymmetrical freedom (the idea that genuine libertas is the power to choose only the good) is developed precisely in conjunction with Augustine’s late doctrines of predestination and grace, not by ignoring them. The clause “while ignoring his thoughts on predestination and grace” therefore renders B factually incorrect.

Hence B is the statement that is not correct.}

7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B}

The question requires identifying the single statement about Augustine's views on free will (and the relevant secondary literature) that is factually inaccurate. Augustine's position evolved from an early emphasis on liberum arbitrium toward a stronger insistence on the necessity of grace after the fall, producing an asymmetrical account of freedom (post-grace humans can will only the good; apart from grace they remain free only to sin). Markus's well-known reading of that asymmetry is standard in the literature; it does not simply “ignore” predestination and grace but rather situates them as the very conditions that make the asymmetry intelligible. Statement B therefore mischaracterizes Markus and is the claim that is not correct.

All other statements are either accurate summaries of Augustine's texts or fair characterizations of the cited interpreters (Gilson, the Calvinist reception, the dialogue with Evodius, etc.).}

