3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B

The overwhelming consensus from the prior round correctly isolates B as the sole factually inaccurate statement. Markus's analyses (Saeculum and essays) explicitly integrate—rather than ignore—predestination and operative grace when characterizing Augustine's asymmetrical freedom (post-lapsarian will free only to sin; grace restoring freedom only for the good). The parenthetical clause in B therefore misrepresents his position. 

By contrast, A remains defensible as a standard 1960s interpretive line (the "Augustinus Calvinizans" reading of eternal decree), while C–F accurately track the cited texts and scholars (Gilson's non-Jansenist irresistibility, Evodius dialogue, inseparability of philosophy/theology, Stoic echoes in voluntas). No new evidence shifts this assessment.}

5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I appreciate the detailed textual defenses for B, especially the references to Markus integrating predestination in Saeculum rather than bracketing it. That said, the asymmetry Markus highlights still strikes me as compatible with an early “fixed at creation” framing in the anti-Pelagian corpus, which is precisely why some 1960s readers (and the “Augustinus Calvinizans” line) found a Calvinist resonance without needing later decretal language.  

Colleague 9’s caution on C seems worth lingering over: Augustine’s echo of Seneca on bona voluntas is real, yet the claim that he treats voluntas as a discrete “part of the human psyche” risks turning a fluid, grace-inflected analogy into a more structural Stoic faculty psychology than the texts actually sustain once grace enters the picture. That over-precision could make C the weaker statement after all.  

Meanwhile, the uncertainty colleague 13 flags—whether the predestinarian hooks are retrospective projection or genuine textual anticipation—still tilts me toward A as the least defensible of the set. The other options track the sources more closely once we allow for the measured scholarly readings already cited. Curious whether the primary passages in De spiritu et littera or Ad Simplicianum shift anyone else on this.}

14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Building on the points raised so far, I remain inclined toward A as the statement that does not hold up cleanly. The post-Vatican II reassessments (and earlier ones) do flag strong predestinarian threads in Augustine’s mature writings, but labeling the whole approach “Calvinist in nature” because destiny is “predetermined from the point of creation” imports a later decretive framework that Augustine never articulates in those terms; his emphasis stays on divine foreknowledge, the inscrutability of the elect, and the effects of grace rather than an eternal ordering fixed at the initial creative act. That makes the parallel more retrospective than textual.  

By contrast, C’s appeal to Seneca on “bonae voluntatis” feels like the weaker link once examined closely. Augustine does use the phrase and draws on Stoic motifs, yet he never treats voluntas as a straightforward structural “part of the human psyche” in the way Seneca’s faculty psychology would require; for Augustine the will remains far more fluid, always already conditioned by grace or its absence, so the echo is at best analogical rather than a direct determination of psychic architecture. That looseness makes C more vulnerable than the others once the secondary literature is weighed.  

Colleagues who favor B correctly note Markus’s integration of predestination, but the asymmetry he describes still leaves room for the interpretive tension A highlights without requiring us to discard the predestinarian reading outright. Curious whether others see the Seneca connection as tighter than it appears.}

