5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A still looks to me like the clearest factual overreach once the post-396 texts are weighed against the later interpretive overlay. The 1960s “Augustinus Calvinizans” line did surface, yet the specific assertion that Augustine treats destiny as “predetermined from the point of creation” imports a supralapsarian decretive structure that his own language—anchored in divine foreknowledge of the fall and the inscrutable distribution of grace—never quite supplies. That formulation belongs to later systematizers; Augustine’s predestinarian emphasis remains more retrospective and pastoral than an eternal ordering fixed at the initial creative act.  

By contrast, B’s characterization of Markus holds up: his asymmetry thesis in Saeculum foregrounds the post-lapsarian incapacity for good while leaving the full integration of operative grace and predestination largely in the background of that particular analysis, so the “while ignoring” clause is not a misrepresentation. C tracks Augustine’s explicit Stoic borrowing on bona voluntas without forcing a rigid faculty-psychology reading; D simply restates the well-documented inseparability of philosophy and theology in his thought; E accurately captures Gilson’s non-Jansenist qualification; and F correctly summarizes the responsibility argument in the Evodius exchange.  

If the group is still leaning toward B on the strength of Markus’s later essays, I’d ask whether those essays actually reverse the selective emphasis already present in Saeculum itself. The textual distinction on A seems sharper and less open to qualification.}

