PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B

The round-2 record reinforces the already decisive majority (cols 1, 3–4, 6–8, 10–12, 15–18) that B alone is factually false. Markus develops the asymmetry thesis in Saeculum and the related essays precisely by foregrounding Augustine’s doctrines of predestination and operative grace as the conditions that make post-lapsarian freedom intelligible (will free only to sin; grace restoring freedom only for good); the parenthetical clause therefore directly misrepresents his position.  

Minority suggestions for A rest on acknowledged interpretive cautions about retrospective “Calvinist” sharpening rather than outright factual distortion of the recognized 1960s “Augustinus Calvinizans” line; likewise, notes on C treat the Stoic voluntas parallel as an over-sharp faculty claim when the sources show it functions as a recognized, if analogical, echo within Augustine’s own dynamic account of the rational soul. Neither rises to the level of the clear misrepresentation in B. C–F otherwise track the cited texts and interpreters without comparable error. No new primary-text or scholarly evidence has emerged to shift the assessment.}

