13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B remains the clearest factual distortion. Markus develops the asymmetry thesis in Saeculum and the related essays precisely by foregrounding the post-lapsarian incapacity for good and the role of operative grace and predestination as the conditions that make that asymmetry intelligible; the parenthetical claim that he proceeds “while ignoring” those doctrines therefore misrepresents the actual argument rather than merely describing a difference of emphasis.  

The minority suggestions that A or C are weaker do not hold up on closer inspection. The post-Vatican II “Augustinus Calvinizans” line in A is a recognized interpretive stance even if its supralapsarian wording invites qualification; it does not introduce the direct factual error about secondary literature that B does. On C, the Senecan echo in bonae voluntatis is textual (Confessions VIII, De spiritu et littera) and Augustine does locate voluntas within the operations of the rational soul; the parallel functions as a classical motif without requiring a rigid faculty-psychology division, so the claim stays within acceptable interpretive latitude. D–F likewise track the primary texts and cited interpreters without comparable distortion.  

The round-1 consensus therefore continues to isolate B as the single statement that does not hold.}

